## 1. Introduction and baseline scope

Our project evaluates control-state optimizations for tiny-gpu, an educational SIMD processor written in SystemVerilog. The baseline has two compute cores, each with four thread lanes, and executes one kernel at a time. Its multicycle instruction schedule separates instruction fetch, decode, operand and memory requests, memory waiting, arithmetic execution, and architectural update. This organization makes the cost of individual control states visible and provides a starting point for reducing execution cycles without changing the instruction set. [R1], [R2]

The midterm establishes the baseline through functional simulation, standard-cell synthesis, and workload-driven power analysis. The selected baseline is the SS 0.75 V / 125 °C clock-gated LVT+HVT implementation in `results/ss/runs/best_ss_lh_cg/`. It was synthesized on the `bender` host, imported through upstream commits `49f7b15`, `cc8ddd5`, and `60003d8`, and relocated without payload changes into the `results/ss/` layout. The earlier TT 0.78 V / 25 °C clock-gated run remains at `results/tt/runs/lh_2.55_cg/` as an exploratory comparison, not as the assignment baseline. Files named `best_lh` and `lh_2.55_final` contain still older ungated or pre-ECO results and are not interchangeable with either baseline. Raw reports take precedence over rounded presentation values. [R3], [R4], [R5]

At the SS corner, the selected design operates at a 2.90 ns clock period, or 344.8 MHz. Design Compiler reports 46,553.590 µm² of pre-ECO cell area; PrimeTime reports 46,632.883 µm² after ECO changes. PrimeTime setup slack is +0.034710 ns and hold slack is +0.000011 ns. Recorded average logic power is 5.2567 mW for matrix multiplication and 5.4775 mW for matrix addition. These values exclude the external program and data memories. The 2.90 ns period is the shortest passing point in the saved DC sweep, but it still carries minimum-pulse-width violations in PrimeTime; Section 5.5 states that limitation explicitly. These PPA values were measured on the netlist synthesized before the two RTL bug fixes in Section 2.2, so they describe the pre-fix implementation. [R5], [R6]

The proposed optimizations are A1, bypassing WAIT for non-memory instructions; A2, merging DECODE and REQUEST; and A3, fusing EXECUTE and UPDATE. They remain implementation plans, not measured RTL improvements.

## 2. Baseline RTL

### 2.1 Architecture and important modules

The RTL is in `rtl/`, relocated from `src/`; three files (`alu.sv`, `core.sv`, `scheduler.sv`) now contain the documented bug fixes. The canonical repository and submission exporter use the required source folder names. The top-level default configuration has eight-bit data, eight-bit memory addresses, 16-bit instructions, four external data-memory channels, and one external program-memory channel. Eight thread lanes share these interfaces through memory controllers. [R2]

| Module | Function in the baseline |
|---|---|
| [`gpu.sv`](../rtl/gpu.sv) | Instantiates the device control register, dispatcher, two cores, and both memory controllers. Registered connections relay LSU requests and responses between cores and the data controller. |
| [`dcr.sv`](../rtl/dcr.sv) | Captures the eight-bit thread count when the host asserts `device_control_write_enable`. |
| [`dispatch.sv`](../rtl/dispatch.sv) | Divides the thread count into blocks of four, assigns blocks to available cores, supplies block IDs and active-lane counts, and asserts kernel completion after all blocks finish. |
| [`controller.sv`](../rtl/controller.sv) | Arbitrates consumer requests onto available memory channels and relays each response to its requesting consumer. Separate instances serve instruction fetches and data loads/stores. |
| [`core.sv`](../rtl/core.sv) | Connects one scheduler, fetcher, and decoder to four lane-local ALUs, LSUs, register files, and PC units. |
| [`scheduler.sv`](../rtl/scheduler.sv) | Sequences FETCH, DECODE, REQUEST, WAIT, EXECUTE, and UPDATE. Tracks block completion and selects the next shared PC. |
| [`fetcher.sv`](../rtl/fetcher.sv) | Requests the instruction at the current PC and holds it until the core consumes it. |
| [`decoder.sv`](../rtl/decoder.sv) | Registers operand addresses, immediates, branch conditions, and control signals during DECODE. Recognizes arithmetic, memory, constant, compare, branch, return, and NOP encodings. |
| [`registers.sv`](../rtl/registers.sv) | Implements 16 eight-bit registers per lane: R0 through R12 are writable; R13, R14, and R15 supply block ID, block size, and thread ID. Reads operands in REQUEST and writes results in UPDATE. |
| [`alu.sv`](../rtl/alu.sv) | Computes ADD, SUB, MUL, DIV, or comparison flags and registers the result during EXECUTE. Division is a combinational eight-bit operation feeding this register. |
| [`lsu.sv`](../rtl/lsu.sv) | Issues a load or store, holds the request while waiting for a response, captures returned data, and resets its transaction state during UPDATE. |
| [`pc.sv`](../rtl/pc.sv) | Maintains lane-local NZP flags and calculates sequential or conditional-branch targets. |

All lanes in a block execute the same instruction. Although each lane calculates a next PC, the scheduler assumes convergence and uses the last active lane's next-PC value. Branch divergence is unsupported. Partial-block behavior therefore needs explicit testing; the two existing workloads use complete four-lane blocks. There is no instantiated cache in the current RTL, despite cache discussion in the original README. [R1], [R2]

### 2.2 Baseline changes and justifications

The latest upstream functional source change before relocation is commit `560688c` from April 2024. The recent project work adds verification, synthesis infrastructure, SS-corner support, and directory organization without implementing A1, A2, or A3. `results/migration.json` and `results/ss/migration.json` record the original and current paths and verify evidence hashes; the three RTL files changed by the fixes retain their original hashes in `historical_sha256`.

| Change | Justification and scope |
|---|---|
| cocotb 2.x compatibility updates, commit `7306732` | Updates Makefile integration, the clock API, and value conversions in trace formatting. This changes the verification environment, not the GPU architecture. |
| Plain-Verilog testbench, now `sim/legacy/tb_gpu.v` | Provides a self-checking top-level DUT testbench usable with both converted RTL and synthesized netlists, without requiring Python during simulation. |
| Four-edge reset in the Verilog testbench, commit `d443022` | Cores receive reset through registered dispatcher signals. Holding reset for several edges avoids capturing unknown core requests when gate-level simulation starts. This is a stimulus fix, not a new hardware feature. |
| `sv2v` conversion to `build/gpu.v` | Converts SystemVerilog arrays and constructs to the Verilog input used by the simulator/synthesis flow. The preserved historical TT input is `results/tt/inputs/gpu.v`; fresh generated conversion stays in `build/`. |
| SS corner selection, commit `49f7b15` | Makes `ss0p75v125c` the default DC/PrimeTime corner and threads the corner, clock-gating flag, and run name through `sweep.sh`. This changes the measurement corner, not the architecture. |
| Multithreshold mapping, clock gating, and timing/DRC ECOs | Changes the synthesized implementation while preserving intended functionality. These are synthesis choices and must be distinguished from source-level bug fixes. |
| SAED32 mux X-pessimism patch, `sim/saed_xfix.awk` | Adds X-select rows to the mux UDP tables used only in gate-level simulation. The SS netlist otherwise holds `core_state` at X after reset because the scheduler's next-state mux select is its own X output while both data inputs agree. This is a simulation-model compatibility fix; it changes no synthesized logic. |
| Scheduler active-lane PC fix, `rtl/scheduler.sv`, `rtl/core.sv` | Bug fix: a partial block previously selected the disabled last lane's stale `next_pc`, restarting at PC 0. The scheduler now receives `thread_count` and selects the last active lane. No ISA change. |
| CMP N/Z/P fix, `rtl/alu.sv` | Bug fix: the unsigned eight-bit subtraction made bit 2 mean "not equal" and bit 0 always zero. The ALU now compares operands directly, producing less-than/equal/greater-than in bits 2/1/0 as documented. No ISA change. |
| Directory reorganization and evidence relocation | Moves sources, testbenches, scripts, and results into the required `rtl/`, `sim/`, `syn/`, `ptpx/`, `sw/`, and `results/` layout. Payload hashes are preserved in the migration manifests. |

The new regression suite exposed two functional defects in the baseline RTL: a partial block (fewer than four active threads) hung because `scheduler.sv` always selected `next_pc[THREADS_PER_BLOCK-1]`, whose lane is disabled and therefore never updates; and `alu.sv` computed CMP flags with an unsigned eight-bit subtraction, so bit 2 meant "not equal" rather than "less than" and bit 0 was never set. These are baseline bugs, not optimization regressions. Both are now fixed: the scheduler receives `thread_count` and selects the last active lane's PC, and the ALU compares the operands directly to produce N/Z/P in bits 2/1/0. The corrected RTL passes the full 1,039-instance RTL corpus (Section 3).

The recorded SS synthesis predates these fixes. Consequently, the SS PPA in Sections 5 and 6 applies to the pre-fix RTL; it must be re-established by resynthesizing the corrected RTL and rerunning PrimeTime. The functional changes are confined to the scheduler state transition and the ALU compare path, but no area or timing claim is made for the corrected netlist until that rerun exists.

A fresh `sv2v` 0.0.13 conversion of the corrected `rtl/` was generated with `make compile` and used for the reported regression; that converted file is retained in `results/verification/gpu.v`. A passing simulation of one conversion does not establish that every future conversion is equivalent.

### 2.3 Memory interface and PPA boundaries

Program memory contains 256 × 16 bits, or 512 bytes; data memory contains 256 × 8 bits, or 256 bytes. Both are testbench arrays outside the synthesized `gpu` module. The Verilog memory model returns registered responses to requests sampled at clock edges and services all four data channels concurrently. It does not model a single-port SRAM's serialization, banking conflicts, macro busy time, or a realistic memory-power cost. [R7]

The memory controllers and core/controller relay registers add latency beyond the memory model itself. The existing waveform shows roughly six cycles in FETCH for the first matmul instruction. A non-memory instruction still visits REQUEST and WAIT before EXECUTE and UPDATE. [R3]

The reported area is standard-cell area, not die area or placed-and-routed core area. The reported power covers synthesized GPU logic, not the external memories or a complete chip. Memory integration will require a separate implementation and measurement boundary.

## 3. Verification

### 3.1 RTL testbench and stimulus generation

The repository has two functional verification paths:

1. The Python/cocotb tests load encoded instructions and data, configure the thread count, drive the memory interface, wait for `done`, and compare output memory with software-calculated results. Supporting files under `sim/legacy/helpers/` provide setup, memory behavior, logging, and cycle traces. [R8]
2. The main Verilog testbench, `tb_gpu`, instantiates `gpu`, generates the clock and reset, initializes program/data arrays, writes the device control register, and launches the selected kernel. Compile-time `MATADD` selects matrix addition; the default is matrix multiplication. `CLK_PERIOD` controls the clock, and `+vcd` enables waveform dumping. [R7]

The original Verilog testbench checks every expected output element, prints a cycle count, and reports `PASS` or mismatches. It also stops a kernel after 100,000 cycles. A limitation is that failure and timeout paths call `$finish`, so a simulator exit code of zero is not sufficient evidence of success. Automated regression must also require an explicit `PASS` and reject `FAIL`, `Mismatch`, and timeout messages.

The new [`sim/tb_regression.v`](../sim/tb_regression.v) and [`sim/run.py`](../sim/run.py) extend verification to the twenty families below. Python generates programs and complete input/expected memory images; the shared testbench supports either converted RTL or a mapped netlist using top-level ports only. It checks all 256 memory bytes, per-address store counts, request stability, unknown payloads, completion, and reset recovery, with `$fatal(1)` on failure. A clean exit plus exactly one matching `PASS <case> cycles=<n>` marker is required for a pass.

During report preparation the suite was compiled with Icarus Verilog 13.0 and run against converted RTL. The first full pass exposed the two baseline defects described in Section 2.2. After fixing them, the full default corpus of 1,039 instances passes at RTL level; the manifest and summary are retained in `results/verification/`. Netlist-mode execution was attempted only in the sense of checking prerequisites: the SAED32 Verilog cell models are not installed in this environment, so no netlist regression result is claimed here.

The cocotb helper currently generates a 25 µs clock. Those tests check functional behavior and are not the source of the 2.90 ns PrimeTime activity traces. The plain-Verilog testbench supplies the period-matched traces.

### 3.2 Netlist simulation

`make gls` compiles the synthesized netlist together with SAED32 standard-cell Verilog models and the same `tb_gpu` testbench. Workload contents and expected results are unchanged. Array ports have already been flattened by conversion/synthesis, matching the packed testbench interfaces. The cell models are preprocessed by [`sim/saed_xfix.awk`](../sim/saed_xfix.awk), which adds X-select rows to mux UDP tables so the SS netlist does not remain at X after reset. That patch affects simulation models only, not the mapped logic. [R7], [R9]

The recorded SS gate-level runs at 2.90 ns report `PASS` and the same 178 cycles for matadd and 491 cycles for matmul as the RTL runs. Their compressed VCDs are retained in `results/ss/activity/`. The current gate-level simulation is zero delay. It does not annotate the saved SDF, so it checks mapped logical behavior and generates switching activity; it does not independently demonstrate setup/hold closure or capture realistic gate-delay glitches. Timing is evaluated separately in Design Compiler and PrimeTime.

The regression testbench can also target a netlist, but no such run is reported: this environment has no SAED32 models or licensed PDK. In addition, the retained netlist is the DC output, while PrimeTime applies its ECO changes only inside the timing/power session. The exact post-ECO netlist has not been exported, so a final netlist regression must use a newly written ECO-applied netlist rather than the archived DC file.

