## 4. Software golden model

### 4.1 Current reference calculations

The cocotb tests calculate expected matrix results in Python. Matadd uses elementwise addition; matmul uses the four corresponding row/column dot products. The Verilog testbench independently calculates the same expected outputs from its initialized input memory. The existing fixed inputs fit in eight bits, so both reference paths agree without needing explicit overflow handling. [R7], [R8]

The original matrix references remain embedded in the older tests. The new [`sw/golden.py`](../sw/golden.py) provides a separate architectural interpreter and instruction encoder for the generated regression programs. It models byte arithmetic, protected metadata, convergent four-lane control flow, and race-free memory accesses, with a bounded instruction budget. It specifies unsigned comparison and N/Z/P in bits 2/1/0, independently of the RTL's expression sizing or flag ordering. Division by zero, divergent branches, invalid instructions, and cross-thread memory races are rejected during generation. Integer result matching is the relevant correctness metric; AI accuracy or image-fidelity metrics do not apply.

### 4.2 Required reference extensions and metrics

For general inputs, the reference must preserve hardware widths: `u8(x) = x & 255` after arithmetic writes, unsigned integer division for nonzero divisors, eight-bit addresses/PCs, and protected metadata registers. CMP/NZP behavior must be specified and checked directly, including SystemVerilog expression sizing and signedness. A reference should not copy an accidental RTL behavior without recording it as a bug or explicit architectural choice.

The implemented reference produces expected final memory and per-address store counts. Generated programs store register signatures so the external-interface testbench can check them without relying on internal hierarchical names. It is not cycle accurate and does not yet emit a full architectural-retirement trace. Software-only unit tests include literal arithmetic/branch expectations, published kernel encodings, independent matrix-product checks, metadata protection, and rejection of nontermination and races. These twenty checks validate suite components; the full RTL corpus is the separate DUT check.

| Metric | Definition and use |
|---|---|
| Functional correctness | Exact equality for every checked output and sentinel location; zero mismatches required. |
| Regression completion | All directed, constrained-random, and corner cases pass at both RTL and gate levels without timeouts or unknown checked outputs. |
| Kernel cycles | Clock edges counted from the testbench's launch interval until `done`; compare only under the same memory model. |
| Kernel latency | `cycles × clock period`; use each implementation's own timing-closed period. |
| Energy estimate | Average measured logic power times kernel latency, with the measurement-window qualification in Section 6.4. |

Software host runtime is not a hardware performance metric. The current kernel reference has no cycle-accurate timing model; cycle counts come from hardware simulation.

## 5. Synthesis

### 5.1 Library corners and constraints

`syn/dc_synth.tcl` now defaults to the assignment's worst-case SS 0.75 V / 125 °C libraries and selects RVT, HVT, and LVT files from one `CORNER` variable. In the selected run, RVT remains available for linking and constraint references but is prohibited from standard-cell mapping. The resulting pre-ECO DC cell mix is 11,563 LVT cells and 3,835 HVT cells, with zero mapped RVT cells. [R11], [R12]

The assignment specifies the following worst-case library files, and the scripts now use exactly these names:

```text
saed32rvt_ss0p75v125c.db
saed32lvt_ss0p75v125c.db
saed32hvt_ss0p75v125c.db
```

The same `CORNER` selection is threaded through Design Compiler, PrimeTime power analysis, and the PrimeTime ECO script. The original SS flow scripts are preserved in `results/ss/flow_snapshot/syn/`; the maintained versions are in `syn/` and `ptpx/`. The earlier TT results are retained only as an exploratory comparison and are not used for any headline PPA number. [R11], [R20]

The constraints in `syn/constraints.sdc` specify an ideal clock, 0.1 ns clock uncertainty, 0.1 ns clock transition, input/output delays equal to 20% of the period, an `INVX1_RVT` input driver, and output load `0.005` in library load units. At 2.90 ns, each I/O delay is 0.58 ns. No physical clock tree or extracted routed interconnect is included. [R13]

### 5.2 Synthesis script and methodology

The flow is top-down synthesis with hierarchy retained using `compile_ultra -no_autoungroup`. It analyzes the converted Verilog, elaborates and links `gpu`, uniquifies instances, checks the design, and reads the timing constraints. The clock-gated configuration uses `compile_ultra -no_autoungroup -gate_clock`. [R11]

The main implementation steps are:

1. Select permitted threshold-voltage libraries. Map critical logic to faster cells while allowing HVT cells elsewhere.
2. Temporarily increase setup uncertainty by 3% of the target period during initial optimization, then restore the stated 0.1 ns uncertainty before final reporting.
3. Insert integrated clock gates for enabled register banks. DC's default gating selection can use RVT gates even when ordinary RVT mapping is disabled, so the script swaps compatible gating cells to allowed LVT cells after compilation. The current design has 211 clock gates.
4. Apply incremental setup recovery, hold fixing, and driver upsizing/buffering. Recheck all three because a hold or capacitance fix can break a near-critical setup path.
5. Write area, timing, QoR, design-rule, power, and Vt-mix reports, followed by the mapped netlist, SDC, SDF, SPEF, and RTL-to-gate name mapping.

DC power reports can use default activity when SAIF is absent; they are not the source of the workload-driven power values reported here. PrimeTime uses the gate-level VCDs instead.

