| 5.25 | 190.5 | +0.04 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 45,705.512 | PASS |
| 6.65 | 150.4 | +0.16 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 44,497.311 | PASS |
| 8.00 | 125.0 | +0.16 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 42,808.016 | PASS |

The shortest DC-passing period in this saved sweep is 2.90 ns. The 2.85 ns candidate has one remaining design-rule net. Area is nonmonotonic across nearby constraints, so the selected 2.90 ns implementation is the best demonstrated DC point, not a proof of a global optimum.

The sweep's pass test does not include PrimeTime's minimum-pulse-width check. At 2.90 ns, PrimeTime reports 877 register clock pins with -0.07 ns pulse-width slack (Section 5.5). The shortest saved period whose ideal half-period minus the 0.1 ns uncertainty exceeds the 1.42 ns library minimum is 3.05 ns (327.9 MHz), but that point has not been carried through PrimeTime ECO and power. The defensible fully closed baseline is therefore still an open item, and 344.8 MHz should be described as the DC-closed / PT-setup-closed point rather than a complete signoff frequency.

For reporting, frequency is `1000 / period_ns` MHz. A final maximum-frequency claim must use the required SS corner and pass independent PrimeTime checks, including pulse width and design rules.

### 5.4 Baseline frequency, area, and critical paths

| Quantity | DC clock-gated design, before PT ECO | PrimeTime design, after ECO |
|---|---:|---:|
| Clock period | 2.90 ns | 2.90 ns |
| Frequency | 344.8 MHz | 344.8 MHz |
| Standard-cell area | 46,553.590 µm² | 46,632.883 µm² |
| Leaf cells | 15,398 | 15,412 |
| Setup slack | +0.03 ns in detailed path report | +0.034710 ns |
| Hold status | Zero violations in QoR | Worst hold slack +0.000011 ns |
| Design-rule status | Zero violating nets in DC QoR | Zero max-capacitance nets; 877 pulse-width violations |
| Clock gates | 211 | 211 |
| Vt mix | 11,563 LVT / 3,835 HVT / 0 RVT | Not separately re-reported |

Sources: DC area/QoR/timing reports and PrimeTime area/summary/constraint reports. The ECO increases cell area by about 79.293 µm². PrimeTime's reported cell area is 0.046633 mm². DC's separate interconnect-area estimate is not added to that number. The leaf-cell count includes clock-gating and buffer/inverter cells. [R5], [R15], [R16], [R18]

The saved DC timing report's critical path is from `lsu_write_valid_reg[2]` in core 0 to `data_memory_controller/mem_write_data_reg[22]`. Data arrival is 2.72 ns and data required is 2.75 ns, giving +0.03 ns slack. The next paths are the same write-valid source into other write-data and write-address bits, with +0.04 ns slack. An arithmetic path from `rt_reg[4]` to `alu_out_reg_reg[0]` also appears near the top of the report. Both memory-control and arithmetic logic therefore constrain the SS implementation, with the LSU write path now dominant. [R17]

The retained PrimeTime setup/hold path reports are filtered to negative slack and therefore contain no path details when timing passes. The positive slack values above come from `summary.rpt`. An unfiltered, high-precision post-ECO critical-path report is still needed to identify the exact PrimeTime worst endpoint and to confirm that the DC critical path remains worst after ECO.

### 5.5 Remaining closure limitations

The PrimeTime ECO script tries stronger drivers or inserted buffers and retains a change only if setup and hold stay nonnegative, or do not worsen an already-negative baseline. At SS, the recorded ECO reduces 172 max-capacitance violations to zero while preserving setup and hold. The ECO increases cell area by 79.293 µm² and leaf-cell count by 14. [R18], [R19]

The remaining signoff problem is minimum pulse width. PrimeTime reports 877 register clock pins with `sequential_clock_pulse_width` violations. Each high-phase check requires 1.42 ns and measures 1.35 ns, for -0.07 ns slack. The arithmetic is transparent: the 2.90 ns clock has a 1.45 ns high phase, and the 0.1 ns clock uncertainty subtracts from the measured pulse, leaving 1.35 ns. The nominal high phase is 0.03 ns above the library minimum, but the team's uncertainty guard band makes the checked value negative. At 3.05 ns the corresponding checked value would be 1.425 ns, just above 1.42 ns; that point exists in the DC sweep but has not been validated in PrimeTime or used for power analysis. The 2.90 ns result should therefore be reported as setup/hold/DRC-net clean but not pulse-width clean.

The very small positive setup slack (+0.034710 ns) and hold slack (+0.000011 ns) leave little nominal margin. All reported timing uses pre-layout wire-load estimates and an ideal clock. It is not post-route signoff. Resolving the pulse-width finding requires either a relaxed period, a clock-waveform change, or library/tool guidance on whether the uncertainty should apply to pulse-width checks; the choice must be documented and re-verified in PrimeTime.

## 6. PrimeTime power analysis

### 6.1 Script and averaged-power methodology

`ptpx/pt_power.tcl` reads a run's netlist and SDC, links the standard-cell libraries, and applies `out/eco_changes.tcl` when present. It updates timing and writes global timing, setup/hold, constraint, and clock reports. It calculates cell area by summing the library areas of leaf cells. The exact SS flow used for the recorded run is preserved in `results/ss/flow_snapshot/syn/`; the TT pre-organization scripts remain in `results/tt/flow_snapshot/syn/`. The maintained script defaults to the SS corner and accepts `CORNER` to reproduce the exploratory TT run. [R20]

The script enables averaged power analysis, reads the workload VCD under the `tb_gpu/dut` hierarchy, updates power, and writes switching-activity, total-power, and hierarchical-power reports. Dynamic power is internal cell power plus net-switching power; leakage is reported separately.

By default, PrimeTime chooses per-hierarchy wire-load estimates rather than reading DC's generated SPEF. The optional `USE_SPEF` flag changes this assumption. The existing baseline must therefore be described as wire-load-based, not extracted-interconnect power/timing analysis.

### 6.2 Workloads, measurement windows, and annotation quality

The activity vectors execute B01 and B02 with the same inputs and 2.90 ns clock period used in verification. Matadd activates both cores; matmul uses one core. This samples two relevant execution patterns, although the fixed small datasets are not a broad workload distribution. The compressed traces are retained under `results/ss/activity/`. [R10]

| Workload | Full VCD / PT averaging window | Nominal launch-to-done interval | Kernel cycles |
|---|---:|---:|---:|
| matmul | 0 to 1,437.675 ns | 1,423.90 ns | 491 |
| matadd | 0 to 529.975 ns | 516.20 ns | 178 |

Both VCDs have a 1 ps timescale. Their final timestamps agree with the simulation windows printed in the corresponding PrimeTime logs. The current script reads the full trace without a workload-only time restriction, so reset and setup activity are included in average power. Each trace spans a complete kernel, but repeated launches and longer steady-state windows are still needed to assess whether the averages are stable over an extended execution period. [R21], [R22]

The final switching reports state that 16,041 of 16,042 nets (99.99%) and all 211 clock-gate outputs have activity-file annotation. The single nonannotated net is `program_memory_controller/mem_write_ready[0]`. This improves on the earlier RTL-VCD mapping approach. However, the power logs contain `PWR-264` VCD identifier/vector-width warnings: 6,789 messages in the matmul log and 5,143 in the matadd log. Each log also records the startup `PT-063` Library Compiler-path error and 167 `NED-079` ECO annotation warnings. Reports were produced, but these diagnostics have not been resolved. [R21], [R22], [R23]

Consequently, 99.99% reported net annotation is a coverage statistic, not proof that every waveform value was interpreted correctly. Before final submission, the team must verify VCD/netlist correspondence, investigate the width warnings and environment error, and rerun power. Because PT applies ECO changes to the loaded design, the final verification flow should also export and simulate that exact ECO-modified netlist and regenerate its VCD. The retained VCDs were generated from the DC netlist, so the reported power and the post-ECO timing/area are not yet measured on the same exact database.

### 6.3 Recorded average power

| Power component (mW) | matmul | matadd |
|---|---:|---:|
| Dynamic: internal plus switching | 1.032396 | 1.204701 |
| Leakage | 4.224331 | 4.272786 |
| Total average logic power | 5.256727 | 5.477488 |
| Leakage fraction | 80.36% | 78.01% |

These are the saved post-ECO SS measurements, subject to the limitations above. The power report rounds the totals to 5.257 mW and 5.477 mW. The reports show no memory-macro power because there are no memory macros inside the measured DUT. Peak power was not measured. [R5], [R6]

The hierarchical matmul report attributes 1.66 mW to core 0, 1.68 mW to the idle core 1, 0.828 mW to the data-memory controller, 0.132 mW to the dispatcher, 0.077 mW to the program-memory controller, and 0.011 mW to the device control register. The remaining approximately 0.87 mW includes top-level relay logic and other blocks. The idle core still has leakage; in this synthesis it is slightly more expensive than the busy core because it received more LVT cells. Clock gating can reduce switching while leaving that leakage largely present. The separate power-group report assigns about 1.041 mW to the clock network for matmul and 1.140 mW for matadd; these are an alternative breakdown, not additional power to add to the totals. [R24]

### 6.4 Derived latency and energy estimates

For a kernel with cycle count `C`, period `T` in ns, and average power `P` in W:

```text
latency_ns = C × T
estimated_energy_nJ = P × latency_ns
```

| Derived metric | matmul | matadd |
|---|---:|---:|
| Launch-to-done latency | 1,423.90 ns | 516.20 ns |
| Energy estimate using full-trace average power | 7.485 nJ | 2.827 nJ |

These energy values multiply a full-trace average by a shorter kernel interval. They are useful first-order estimates, not direct integrations over the active-kernel window. Exact kernel energy requires a consistently delimited power window or time-resolved integration. No external-memory energy is included.

## 7. Validation of PPA and software metrics

### 7.1 Validation against the original design documentation

The original project README documents eight-bit data, 16-bit instructions, lane-local metadata registers, the multicycle schedule, and the two matrix kernels. Its expected matadd output and standard 2×2 matrix-product calculation agree with the checks in Section 3.3. Inspection of the top-level RTL confirms the two-core/four-lane configuration used in this report. The current RTL, rather than README discussion of unfinished cache features, defines the implementation boundary. [R1], [R2]

The software calculations provide an independent mathematical check of final kernel outputs. Fresh converted-RTL simulations reproduce those outputs and the recorded 178/491-cycle counts. The new regression suite goes further: it executes all twenty families against converted RTL and, after the two fixes described in Section 2.2, reports 1,039 of 1,039 default instances passing. The suite initially exposed those defects, so it is consistent with the reference and the documented ISA rather than tuned to the RTL. This still does not validate the full instruction set beyond the exercised programs or all legal launch conditions.

### 7.2 Cross-checks on reported PPA

The following checks support internal consistency:

- `1000 / 2.90 = 344.8276` MHz, which rounds to 344.8 MHz.
- `46,632.883 / 1,000,000 = 0.046632883` mm².
- In both PT summaries, dynamic plus leakage power equals total power to the displayed precision.
- The saved VCD endpoints (1,437.675 ns and 529.975 ns) match the full averaging windows in the PT logs.
- The original DC implementation and the PT ECO implementation have different cell counts and areas; using their respective reports explains the difference rather than attributing it to a unit conversion. The ECO adds 79.293 µm² and 14 leaf cells.
- DC and PrimeTime both report no setup or hold violations at 2.90 ns. PrimeTime additionally reports 877 minimum-pulse-width violations, which the DC QoR report does not check.
- The SS corner costs about 12.1% in frequency and 8.5% in cell area relative to the exploratory TT result (392.2 MHz, 42,974.734 µm²). Those are different measurement corners, not an optimization.

The earlier RVT run in `results/tt/initial_rvt/` has 35,257.398 µm² cell area at a 10 ns target. That is an initial mapping point, not a measured RVT maximum frequency. Describing 344.8 MHz as a demonstrated 3.4× gain over the RVT design's maximum frequency would therefore overstate the evidence. [R25]

### 7.3 External validation and memory estimates

The original design documentation validates architecture and workload semantics, but it does not provide a directly comparable SAED32 PPA reference. No independently matched published PPA result is established here. A comparison against a different GPU, technology node, voltage, memory boundary, or placed-and-routed implementation would need those differences stated explicitly.

The project slides estimate about 25,899 µm² for one 256×8 data SRAM plus two 256×8 program SRAMs, approximately 55.5% of the reported SS logic area. They also quote 6 ns macro cycle time and 3 ns access time, and discuss a four-bank data-memory alternative. These are planning estimates, not measurements of the present DUT. [R3], [R4]

The SRAM inspection script targets a separate TT 1.05 V macro library, whereas the measured standard-cell logic uses SS 0.75 V. The underlying macro datasheet and complete probe results are not included with this report. We therefore do not add the slide's macro area or standby-power estimate to the logic PPA or claim that it validates the current memory system. Macro timing, supply compatibility, port count, and power need direct source verification before integration. [R26]

## 8. Proposed optimizations: implementation plan

### 8.1 A1: bypass WAIT for non-memory instructions

The baseline unconditionally transitions from REQUEST to WAIT. A1 will change `scheduler.sv` so a non-memory instruction proceeds directly from REQUEST to EXECUTE; LDR and STR will retain the existing wait protocol. Decoded memory enables are already available at this point. Operand capture in REQUEST and result writeback in UPDATE will remain unchanged.

Implementation should first compare the baseline and A1 state traces for an arithmetic instruction, a load, and a store. Non-memory instructions should lose one state cycle without issuing a memory transaction. Memory instructions must still wait for every required LSU response, and RET must still assert completion only after its architectural effects are finished.

Matmul executes 41 dynamic instructions per block, of which five are loads/stores. The initial estimate is therefore 36 saved cycles: 491 to approximately 455. Matadd has 13 instructions per block and three loads/stores, giving an estimate of ten saved cycles: 178 to approximately 168. These estimates assume unchanged memory timing and no new inter-core arbitration effects.

### 8.2 A2: merge DECODE and REQUEST

A2 will make decode information available before operand capture at a merged decode/request edge. The present decoder and register-file reads are synchronous. Simply renaming or skipping a state would cause consumers to see stale decoded addresses or controls because nonblocking register updates become visible after the edge.

The proposed implementation will:

1. Separate combinational instruction decoding from any controls that need to be held during execution.
2. Capture register operands and stable execution controls at the merged-state edge.
3. Preserve the LSU's REQUESTING step so request addresses/data are driven only after the newly captured operands are valid.
4. Update fetcher-consumption and scheduler state checks consistently in `decoder.sv`, `registers.sv`, `lsu.sv`, `fetcher.sv`, `scheduler.sv`, and their connections in `core.sv`.
5. Test arithmetic dependencies, immediate changes, load/store sequences, and CMP followed by branch before measuring cycle savings.

The initial estimate is one saved cycle per dynamic instruction: 41 for matmul and 13 for matadd. The added decode-to-operand/control logic can increase the critical path, so the estimate alone is not an acceptance criterion.

### 8.3 A3: fuse EXECUTE and UPDATE

A3 will evaluate a combined execution/writeback stage. In the current RTL, EXECUTE registers `alu_out` and next-PC information, while UPDATE consumes those registered values. Combining their state encodings without changing the datapath would write the previous result.

The implementation must expose the current instruction's combinational arithmetic result and branch/PC decision to the architectural writeback edge, or provide an equivalent bypass. CMP flag writes, branch evaluation, load-result selection, RET handling, and LSU cleanup must all observe the correct instruction. This requires coordinated changes in `alu.sv`, `pc.sv`, `registers.sv`, `scheduler.sv`, and `lsu.sv`.

The main timing risk is the combinational divider and the longer path into register-file writeback. Since the SS baseline has only +0.034710 ns setup margin at 2.90 ns, A3 must be resynthesized and evaluated at its own closed period. It should be kept only if total execution latency or energy improves under the project's stated area/power constraints.

### 8.4 Evaluation order and acceptance criteria

First close the two baseline defects and complete the required SS-corner flow, then freeze that corrected baseline. Evaluate A1 alone, A2 alone, A1+A2, A3 alone, and finally any combination justified by their individual results. This order isolates causes of failures and prevents synthesis-flow improvements from being counted as RTL cycle reductions.

| Estimated change at unchanged 2.90 ns period | matmul | matadd |
|---|---:|---:|
| A1 cycle reduction | 36 (7.33%) | 10 (5.62%) |
| A2 cycle reduction | 41 (8.35%) | 13 (7.30%) |
| A1+A2 estimated cycles | 414 | 155 |
| A1+A2 estimated cycle reduction | 15.68% | 12.92% |
| A1+A2 estimated latency | 1,200.60 ns | 449.50 ns |

These are hypotheses, not preliminary measured optimization results. At unchanged average power, the corresponding energy estimates would be about 6.31 nJ for matmul and 2.46 nJ for matadd. Actual power and period must be remeasured. For A1+A2 to beat baseline latency, its period must remain below approximately 3.439 ns for matmul and 3.330 ns for matadd, using the current cycle estimates. Those thresholds are computed as baseline latency divided by optimized cycles; they will change again after the baseline bugs are fixed and the design is resynthesized.

An accepted variant must pass all functional regressions, satisfy setup/hold and design-rule constraints, and improve measured kernel latency and/or energy without silently changing workload or memory assumptions. Its ISA, output data, and supported convergent control flow must remain unchanged. FETCH latency is a large remaining cost that A1 through A3 do not directly eliminate.

## 9. Proposed optimizations: verification plan

### 9.1 Regression and architectural checks

For each variant, run B01/B02 plus all 1,039 default R01-R10 and C01-C10 instances on both RTL and the final mapped netlist. The corrected baseline passes 1,039 of 1,039 at RTL level; the netlist run still has to be repeated on a corrected, ECO-applied netlist. Save the testcase ID, seed, memory timing parameters, expected and actual output signatures, cycle count, and pass/fail log. A failure must return a failing process status as well as a diagnostic message.

Add checks that disabled lanes do not generate writes, outstanding memory requests retain stable address/data until acknowledged, the scheduler does not commit a load before its result is valid, and `done` follows the completion of all requested blocks. The monitor must respect the existing level-held valid/ready protocol rather than treating every high-valid cycle as a separate new transaction.

For control-stage changes, compare architectural retirement events or committed results, not raw cycle-by-cycle internal signals. A faster implementation will intentionally change state occupancy and memory-request timing. Compare transaction content and per-thread ordering without requiring identical arbitration order for independent accesses.

### 9.2 Golden-model updates

A1 through A3 preserve instruction semantics, so the functional model should stay unchanged. If an optimization requires altering expected numerical outputs, it is not an accepted implementation of these proposals. The model's timing assumptions and performance monitor must change to count the new stages and memory waits; any cycle-accurate model added later must identify which hardware version it describes.

If testing reveals a baseline defect in comparison flags, partial-block execution, reset, or another existing function, first define the intended behavior, add a failing regression, and document the bug fix. Freeze new baseline measurements after that fix before comparing optimized RTL.

The current suite exposed two such defects, and both are now fixed and re-verified:

1. **Partial-block PC selection.** `scheduler.sv` updated `current_pc` from `next_pc[THREADS_PER_BLOCK-1]`. For a block with fewer than four active threads, that lane's `pc` instance is disabled by `enable = i < thread_count`, so its `next_pc` never left zero and execution restarted at PC 0 forever. The scheduler now receives `thread_count` and selects `next_pc[thread_count-1]`, with PC 0 held for the zero-thread case. C01 and C03_threads_13 pass, and the full-block workloads are unchanged.
2. **CMP flag encoding.** `alu.sv` computed `(rs - rt > 0)`, `(rs - rt == 0)`, and `(rs - rt < 0)` on an eight-bit unsigned subtraction. The difference wrapped for `rs < rt`, so bit 2 became "not equal" rather than "less than" and the bit-0 less-than term was always false. The ALU now compares the operands directly and writes N/Z/P to bits 2/1/0 for `a < b`, `a == b`, and `a > b`. C09 and R09 pass, and the matmul `BRn` loop still iterates the same way.

Both fixes change only control/compare logic and do not add ISA features. They have been validated at RTL level by the full 1,039-instance corpus. They must still be followed by a fresh SS synthesis, PrimeTime ECO, VCD regeneration, power measurement, and a new full regression on the exact post-ECO netlist. The PPA in Sections 5 and 6 predates these fixes and must not be presented as applying to the corrected RTL until that rerun is complete.

### 9.3 Memory latency, contention, and waveform inspection

Extend the testbench to sweep response latency over 1, 2, 4, and 8 cycles, plus independent finite channel delays. Evaluate ideal concurrent data channels separately from a serialized single-port memory and any proposed banked model. Identical data capacity does not imply identical bandwidth.

For the slide's tentative 6 ns cycle / 3 ns access SRAM model at a 2.90 ns logic clock, the rounded minimum access spacing is three cycles and read availability requires at least two logic-clock intervals, before wrapper/controller delays. Exact handshake timing must be defined at clock edges and validated against the actual macro specification. Do not reuse ideal-memory cycle counts for this different model.

Inspect waveforms around non-memory WAIT bypass, the merged decode/request edge, load completion, CMP/branch dependencies, reset recovery, and final RET. The current first-instruction waveform provides a baseline example:

![Baseline matmul first instruction: core FSM and instruction-fetch handshake](../docs/img/waveform_matmul_first_instr.png)

### 9.4 PPA and energy re-evaluation

Repeat the period search at SS for every accepted variant using the same I/O/load assumptions and permitted Vt choices. Export the exact post-ECO netlist and constraints, run gate-level regression on that implementation, regenerate period-matched workload activity, resolve annotation warnings, and measure both kernels in PrimeTime.

For average power, repeat enough launches that extending the measurement window produces a stable result, with the convergence criterion chosen and reported in advance. Report reset-inclusive and kernel-only windows separately if both matter. For each workload, record cycles, closed period, latency, area, internal/switching/leakage power, and consistently delimited energy. Include the memory implementation in PPA only when it is actually part of the evaluated design.

